      <h2>In-Depth Carrier Analysis: Who Should Choose Whom?</h2>

      <h3>1. Erie Insurance: Best Overall for Low Rates & Rate Lock</h3>
      <p>
        Operating across 12 Mid-Atlantic and Midwestern states (including PA, OH, VA, NC, MD, IL), Erie Insurance is consistently the lowest-cost private passenger auto insurer. Their signature <strong>Erie Rate Lock®</strong> feature prevents your rate from increasing until you add or remove a vehicle, add or remove a driver, or change your residential address—even if you file an accident claim.
      </p>

      <h3>2. GEICO: Best Cheap Rates for Clean Drivers & Tech Convenience</h3>
      <p>
        GEICO leverages its lean direct-to-consumer digital infrastructure to keep underwriting overhead lower than agency-heavy competitors. They offer substantial affinity discounts (up to 15%) for federal employees, military personnel, and members of over 800 professional and alumni associations.
      </p>

      <h3>3. State Farm: Best Cheap Rates for Drivers with Moving Violations</h3>
      <p>
        While State Farm’s clean-record pricing is slightly higher than GEICO’s, their actuarial model penalizes minor speeding tickets and fender benders far less harshly. If you have an imperfect driving record or need to insure a teenage driver, State Farm’s <em>Steer Clear®</em> young driver discount produces the lowest blended household rate.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Progressive: Best for High-Risk Drivers & Instant Budget Matching</h3>
      <p>
        Progressive is the industry pioneer in underwriting higher-risk motorists. If you require an SR-22 filing following a major infraction or suspension, Progressive’s specialty casualty division is significantly more accommodating than standard mutual carriers. Their <em>Name Your Price®</em> tool allows drivers to calibrate coverage tiers directly to their monthly cash flow.
      </p>

      <h2>The Dangerous "Cheap Insurance" Traps to Avoid</h2>
      <p>
        Pursuing the lowest price is sensible, but policyholders must avoid cutting the wrong corners:
      </p>

      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Three Costly Underinsurance Traps:</h4>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Buying Only State Statutory Minimums:</strong> If you cause a multi-vehicle pileup that totals two new $45,000 electric vehicles, a state minimum property damage limit of $15,000 or $25,000 leaves you personally on the hook for the remaining $65,000 judgment—putting your home equity and wages at risk.</li>
          <li><strong>Rejecting Uninsured Motorist Coverage:</strong> Declining UM/UIM coverage saves only $4 to $8 per month, but leaves you completely unprotected if an uninsured motorist hits you and flees the scene.</li>
          <li><strong>Selecting Subprime "Direct Mail" Insurers:</strong> Fly-by-night non-standard carriers may advertise $39/month rates, but frequently maintain F ratings with the Better Business Bureau, delay claims payments for months, and use substandard aftermarket collision parts.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <div class="faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Cheap Auto Insurance</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is the absolute cheapest way to insure a car? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            The cheapest legal way is to carry state minimum liability on a fully paid-off, reliable older vehicle (dropping comprehensive and collision), while taking advantage of telematics, paperless billing, and multi-policy discounts.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Why did my car insurance rate go up if I had no accidents? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Insurers apply general rate revisions approved by state insurance commissioners to entire ZIP code territories to offset rising medical care inflation, litigation costs, and severe weather loss trends. Additionally, carriers employ "price optimization" which gradually tests price elasticity on unengaged policyholders.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Author Box -->
      <div class="author-card">
        <div class="author-avatar">MV</div>
        <div class="author-info">
          <h4>Reviewed by Marcus Vance, CPCU</h4>
          <span class="author-title">Senior Property & Casualty Underwriting Analyst</span>
          <p class="author-bio">
            Marcus is a Chartered Property Casualty Underwriter (CPCU) with 18+ years analyzing competitive rate filings, consumer shopping behavior, and insurance carrier loss ratios.
          </p>
        </div>
